WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.032
Retrieval-augmented generation is a long name for an open-book way of answering a question.

00:00:07.032 --> 00:00:11.143
First, find the parts of a document that might answer it.

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:13.953
Retrieval is the name for that search.

00:00:13.953 --> 00:00:19.949
Next, put the selected passages together with the question so the model can read both.

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:22.610
This is what augmented means here.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:28.565
You are giving the model material for this answer; you do not need to teach it the whole book.

00:00:28.565 --> 00:00:33.337
Finally, the model writes a reply using the text it received.

00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:41.007
A good way to check the reply is to put your finger on the sentence in the source that supports each part.

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:44.169
Let's walk through it with Orbit's policy file.

00:00:44.169 --> 00:00:46.446
You start with your documents.

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:54.693
Ahead of time, you split them into chunks, small passages that make sense on their own, like one section of the policy.

00:00:54.693 --> 00:01:00.455
When a question arrives, you score every chunk against it and keep the best two or three.

00:01:00.455 --> 00:01:04.247
Those chunks go into the prompt, together with the question.

00:01:04.247 --> 00:01:09.615
The model answers from them, or says plainly that they don't cover the question.

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:15.333
Splitting happens once. Searching and answering happen for every question.

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:19.007
Owen bought a jacket for three hundred and ten dollars.

00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:23.884
He asks whether he can return it and whether he must pay return shipping.

00:01:23.884 --> 00:01:29.710
That sounds like one question, but try answering it from the return deadline alone.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.703
Knowing how many days he has tells you nothing about the shipping charge.

00:01:34.703 --> 00:01:41.840
Search Orbit’s policy for two things: the conditions for a return and the rule about shipping fees.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:50.067
The first passage says unused items in their original packaging can come back within thirty days of delivery.

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:55.805
The second says return shipping is free for orders over seventy-five dollars.

00:01:55.805 --> 00:01:59.414
Supply both passages with Owen’s question.

00:01:59.414 --> 00:02:03.066
How do you score the chunks? There are two common ways.

00:02:03.066 --> 00:02:08.892
The simple way is keyword scoring: count the words each chunk shares with the question.

00:02:08.892 --> 00:02:15.402
It needs no extra service and fits in a few lines of Python. It's what you will build today.

00:02:15.402 --> 00:02:21.866
Its weakness is synonyms. Send it back and return mean the same thing, but share no words.

00:02:21.866 --> 00:02:28.812
A second approach uses an embedding, a set of numbers that represents some of the meaning in a passage.

00:02:28.812 --> 00:02:38.746
The question can be represented the same way. This lets a search for send it back find a return policy even when those exact words are missing.

00:02:38.746 --> 00:02:46.993
Those numbers live in a vector store, such as Chroma or pgvector, which searches millions of chunks quickly.

00:02:46.993 --> 00:02:50.282
Draw a line through the middle of Owen's message.

00:02:50.282 --> 00:02:54.444
On one side he asks whether he can return the jacket.

00:02:54.444 --> 00:02:57.818
On the other he asks who pays to ship it back.

00:02:57.818 --> 00:03:02.096
One relevant passage might settle only half the message.

00:03:02.096 --> 00:03:08.103
Place the return conditions beside the first question and the fee rule beside the second.

00:03:08.103 --> 00:03:11.020
His order value helps with the fee rule.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:17.710
It tells you nothing about whether the jacket is unused or still in its original packaging.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:22.105
Cover the fee passage and read the proposed reply again.

00:03:22.105 --> 00:03:25.224
You can still discuss the return conditions.

00:03:25.224 --> 00:03:27.853
You now lack a source for free shipping.

00:03:27.853 --> 00:03:33.594
This little test shows exactly what retrieving the second passage contributed.

00:03:33.594 --> 00:03:37.043
Now construct the reply one claim at a time.

00:03:37.043 --> 00:03:43.924
Owen’s purchase is above seventy-five dollars, so the shipping rule supports free return shipping.

00:03:43.924 --> 00:03:49.900
To confirm his jacket qualifies, you still need its condition and delivery date.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.231
Explain the rule and ask for missing facts.

00:03:53.231 --> 00:03:59.225
Two good passages answer more, but they cannot supply facts Owen never gave.
